- What is an IAM Role
It is an identity you can create in IAM that has specific permissions. A role is similar to a user, as it is an AWS Identity with permission policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS
However, instead of being uniquely associated with one person, a role is intended to be assumable by anyone who needs it 

- Roles are Temporary
It does not have standard long term credentials the same way passwords or access keys do. Instead when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.

- What else can Roles do
Roles can be assumed by people, AWS architecture, or other system-level accounts
It can allow cross account access. This allows one AWS account the ability to interact with resources in other AWS account

- Console Demo
Create IAM Role - Ensure it has S3 access
Create an EC2 instance - Attach the role we just created
Create S3 bucket - Using CLI
Access S3 from EC2 Instance

IAM > Create Role > AWS Service for EC2 > S3FullAccess > Create
EC2 > Create Instance > Select key pair and security group > Launch > Select Instance > Actions > Security > Modify IAM Role > Attach above and update. It will be added in Security tab
Connect to EC2 > sudo su, aws s3 ls > No Access keys. Boooom

Creds are stored in EC2 and hackers can get that, but by Roles they can only assume role

- Tips
Roles are preferred from a security perspective
Avoids hard-coding credentials
Policies control a roles permission
You can update a policy attached to a role, and it will take immediate effect
You can attach or detach roles to running EC2 instances without having to stop or terminate those instances
